With `FORCE_HTTPS=true`, a request that reaches the API looking like plain `http` with a `Host` other than the one in `PUBLIC_API_URL` is rejected with `400` and the body `{"error":"FORCE_HTTPS_NON_CANONICAL_HOST"}`. The API request log shows the same thing as `reason=force_https_non_canonical_host` on the `-->` line. Browsers hide the status and body of a failed WebSocket upgrade, so from the page it only reads as "failed to connect".

Check that your proxy:

- forwards `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` on WebSocket upgrade requests, not just plain requests;
- preserves the original `Host` header on upgrades (nginx: `proxy_set_header Host $host;`);
- has its IP in `TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS`, otherwise the API ignores the forwarded headers.

The API also logs a rate-limited `[force-https]` warning on this path with the values it saw.

If you prefer nginx, here's an equivalent configuration.

**The upstream ports below are not published by default:**
  The shipped `docker-compose.yml` publishes host ports for **Caddy only** (`80` and `443`). The `api` (3001), `web` (4321) and `portal` (4322) containers are reachable on the compose network but not from the host, so an nginx (or Nginx Proxy Manager) upstream of `127.0.0.1:4322` returns **502** until you either:

  - **Front Caddy instead** (recommended) — point nginx at `http://127.0.0.1:80` as a single upstream with WebSocket support, and set `CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS=:80` in `.env` so Caddy stays in plain-HTTP mode behind your TLS terminator. Caddy already routes `/api/*`, `/portal/*` and the dashboard, and it is the configuration tested every release. This is the same arrangement as [Cloudflare Tunnel, Option A](/deploy/cloudflare-tunnel/).
  - **Publish the ports** — add a `docker-compose.override.yml` with `ports:` entries for `api` (`3001:3001`), `web` (`4321:4321`) **and** `portal` (`4322:4322`), then use the per-path config below. If you publish only api and web, everything works except `/portal`, which 502s.

  **nginx + certbot:**
```nginx
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name breeze.yourdomain.com;
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }

    server {
        listen 443 ssl http2;
        server_name breeze.yourdomain.com;

        ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/breeze.yourdomain.com/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/breeze.yourdomain.com/privkey.pem;
        ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

        # Security headers
        add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;
        add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
        add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
        add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;

        # API routes
        location /api/ {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3001;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

            # WebSocket support
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
            proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
            proxy_read_timeout 86400s;
        }

        location /health {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3001;
        }

        # Customer portal (Astro SSR on :4322 under the /portal base path). This
        # mirrors Caddy's `path /portal /portal/*`: an exact match for /portal
        # plus a prefix match for everything under it (so it can't also grab an
        # unrelated path like /portalX). The portal emits /portal-prefixed URLs,
        # so keep the prefix — no trailing slash on proxy_pass. Its API calls
        # stay on the /api/ block above.
        location = /portal {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:4322;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }

        location /portal/ {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:4322;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }

        # Web dashboard
        location / {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:4321;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }
    }
```

  When using nginx instead of Caddy, remove the `caddy` service from `docker-compose.yml` and set `TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=true` in your `.env`.

**Trust Center embeds and proxy-added framing headers:**
  The server-level `add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;` above also lands on `/portal/trust/embed/` responses, the one portal path that may be framed by the websites a [Trust Center embed](/security/trust-center-embed/) allows. A proxy that adds its own `Content-Security-Policy` to those responses always breaks embedding, because a second, restrictive CSP is enforced alongside the portal's. A proxy-added `X-Frame-Options` is ignored by modern browsers when the portal's `frame-ancestors` is present, but it still blocks the embed in older browsers. Exclude `location /portal/trust/embed/` from both, for example with its own `location` block that sets only the headers you need (nginx does not inherit server-level `add_header` into a block that declares its own).

Some Claude models decline requests that resemble harmful work. The category is reported with the refusal, for example `cyber` for malware analysis or exploit-like scripts. Security triage in an RMM can trigger this even when the work is legitimate.

When a model declines, Breeze:

- records the refusal and its category against the model in AI usage;
- shows the technician "The model declined this request (category: …)", lists the other models they are allowed to switch to, and links here;
- ends an AI agent run as **Blocked — model refused**, with the category in the run outcome.

Each feature's default model can have an ordered list of up to five **backup models** (**Partner Settings → AI Providers & Models → Defaults by feature**). Breeze moves to the next usable backup when the model:

- is disabled or retired, or its connection stops working;
- is rate-limited, overloaded or returns a server error;
- rejects its API key, or the provider account is out of quota.

Backups follow the same rules as the default: the feature's allowed models, plan, data residency and tool support. A backup that was just tried, or that is cooling down after a recent failure, is skipped. A model cools down for about a minute after a rate limit, overload or server error, and for up to 15 minutes after a key or quota failure. Replacing the connection's API key clears that wait at once. If every backup is unusable, Breeze still uses the default rather than failing the feature.

A timeout is never a reason to fail over, because the provider may still have run the request. A model you picked yourself in the chat model menu is never swapped for a backup.

