Because a desktop session is peer-to-peer, screen and input traffic flows straight between the viewer and the device once the connection is up — the Breeze server is no longer in that path. A **session lease** keeps the server in control anyway.

The agent holds a short lease and renews it about every 25 seconds over its existing control connection. Each renewal re-checks, live, that the technician is still allowed to be there:

- the session is still open and the user account is still active;
- the user is still a member of the customer organisation the device belongs to;
- their role, role permissions, and site scope have not changed since the session started;
- the device is still inside their site scope;
- multi-factor authentication is still satisfied under the current role policy;
- the session's hard time limit has not been reached.

The MFA check evaluates the role's *actual, effective* MFA policy, not just its raw flag — a Partner Admin who hasn't enrolled an MFA factor no longer has their desktop sessions revoked when MFA isn't actually required by policy.

If any of those checks fails, the session ends within about half a minute: the agent stops capture and input, the viewer shows **Session ended: access revoked**, and the session record is marked disconnected. Removing a technician's membership or changing their role now ends their live sessions immediately as well, rather than only blocking the next one.

With agents from this release on, the WebSocket (JPEG) fallback keeps the same lease: the agent renews it over the same connection and stops capturing when it is revoked or lapses, exactly as on WebRTC. Older agents do not hold a lease on this transport; for them the server re-checks the session about every 30 seconds and stops relaying frames and input when it no longer passes. With any agent version, ending a session (**End**, or any server-side teardown) stops relaying a WebSocket-fallback session at once, rather than at the relay's next half-minute check.

If the server is briefly unreachable, the session is **not** dropped — the agent tolerates about 90 seconds of failed renewals before ending the session on its own. That way a short API or network hiccup does not disconnect anyone, while a device that genuinely cannot reach Breeze cannot hold a remote-control session open indefinitely.

  **Agent update required.** Only agents that support session leases and the session start fence (v0.114.0 and later) can start a remote desktop session. If you click **Connect → Desktop** and see *"Remote desktop needs an agent update on this device"*, the agent has not yet reported lease support — agents update themselves automatically, so this normally clears within one heartbeat interval (about a minute). If you pin agent versions in a self-hosted deployment, update the pinned build before your technicians will be able to start desktop sessions again. Remote Terminal, the Files tab, and every other remote tool are unaffected.

