`PATCH /users/:id` updates a user's **name**, **status**, or `disabledReason`. These fields live on the shared global identity row, which can be linked to memberships in multiple partners or organisations, so no tenant-scoped authorization proof can cover every tenant a change might affect (including revoking sessions). The endpoint is therefore restricted to **system-scoped callers** (`auth.scope === 'system'`) regardless of `users:write` -- a partner or organisation admin cannot call it directly. For example, disabling a user:

```json
{
  "status": "disabled"
}
```

Every response includes the following security headers:

```
Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
X-Content-Type-Options:    nosniff
X-Frame-Options:           DENY
Referrer-Policy:           strict-origin-when-cross-origin
Permissions-Policy:        camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
Content-Security-Policy:
    default-src 'self';
    script-src 'self';
    style-src 'self';
    img-src 'self' data: blob:;
    font-src 'self';
    connect-src 'self' wss://*.2breeze.app;
    frame-ancestors 'none';
    base-uri 'self';
    form-action 'self'
```

Capability URLs, where the address itself is the secret, send `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` instead of the default: the caller-verification links under `/api/v1/verify/*` and the web `/verify/<token>` page, public invoice and payment links, and email unsubscribe links. The route's own value wins over the application default, and `docker/Caddyfile.prod` sets the global `Referrer-Policy` only when the upstream response carries none (`?Referrer-Policy`), so a self-maintained Caddy config must do the same or it will overwrite the stricter value.

`script-src`/`style-src` never carry `'unsafe-inline'` in production, regardless of `CSP_ALLOW_UNSAFE_INLINE` — that override only applies outside production. The `connect-src` allowlist comes from `CSP_CONNECT_HOSTS` (comma-separated `wss://` hosts, e.g. `wss://*.2breeze.app,wss://us.2breeze.app`); self-hosters should set it to their own domain(s) — it falls back to Breeze's own `wss://*.2breeze.app` wildcard if unset.

```js
import { createRemoteJWKSet, jwtVerify } from 'jose';

const origin = 'https://breeze.example.com';
const page = `${origin}/portal/trust/northwind/acme`;
const jwks = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${origin}/api/v1/trust/attestation/jwks.json`));

const token = await (await fetch(`${origin}/api/v1/trust/attestation/northwind/acme`)).text();
const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, jwks, {
  algorithms: ['ES256'],
  typ: 'trust-attestation+jwt',
  issuer: origin,
  subject: page,
});
console.log(payload.trust.lastEvaluatedAt, payload.trust.payload.summary);
```

```csharp
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

var origin = "https://breeze.example.com";
var page = $"{origin}/portal/trust/northwind/acme";
using var http = new HttpClient();
var jwks = new JsonWebKeySet(await http.GetStringAsync($"{origin}/api/v1/trust/attestation/jwks.json"));
var token = await http.GetStringAsync($"{origin}/api/v1/trust/attestation/northwind/acme");

var result = await new JsonWebTokenHandler().ValidateTokenAsync(token, new TokenValidationParameters
{
    IssuerSigningKeys = jwks.GetSigningKeys(),
    ValidAlgorithms = new[] { SecurityAlgorithms.EcdsaSha256 },
    ValidTypes = new[] { "trust-attestation+jwt" },
    ValidIssuer = origin,
    ValidateAudience = false, // the token has no aud claim
    ValidateLifetime = true,
});
if (!result.IsValid || (string)result.Claims["sub"] != page)
    throw new SecurityTokenException("attestation did not verify");
```

A token stays verifiable after it expires, as long as its key is still in the JWKS: keys that are rotated out remain published as verify-only. An expired token is a **historical** statement ("on this date, this installation computed this payload"), not a statement about now. To check an archived token, verify the signature, `typ`, `iss` and `sub` as above, and evaluate the dates as of the time it was issued instead of now. With `jose`:

```js
import { decodeJwt, jwtVerify } from 'jose'; // plus `jwks`, `origin` and `page` from the Node example above

const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, jwks, {
  algorithms: ['ES256'], typ: 'trust-attestation+jwt', issuer: origin, subject: page,
  currentDate: new Date(decodeJwt(token).iat * 1000), // dates checked as of issuance
});
```

For a current statement, fetch a new token. A key that was revoked after a compromise is removed from the JWKS, and tokens it signed no longer verify.
